WHAT WAS TAKEN. Eight glyph outlines, in one module.

`src/data/addressMarkerIcons.js` draws the ADEME energy-performance grade of a
building as a letter in a frame. The letters are Inter's:

    A B C D E F G   — the seven grades of the official DPE ladder
    ?               — the case the register publishes with no grade at all

Extracted with fontkit from the variable font at the named instance
**wght 700, opsz 14**. The optical-size axis is set to 14 rather than 32 on
purpose: Inter's small-size design opens the counters, and these draw at 15 CSS
px on the globe, where a closed bowl turns an A into a triangle.

WHY A TYPEFACE AND NOT A DRAWING. The eight used to be stroked paths authored in
that module — even line weight, arcs where a typeface has curves, no relation
between one letter and the next beyond a shared bounding box. They read as
traced rather than set. Everything else in this project's iconography is a
picture of an object; a letter is not. It is a character, and characters are the
work of type designers.

Inter specifically because this application already sets its whole interface in
it (`--font-sans` in `style.css`, loaded from Google Fonts in `index.html`), so
a grade badge on the globe and the same grade printed on its card are now the
same letterforms.

WHAT WAS CHANGED. No control point.

Each glyph's `d` is stored exactly as the font contains it, in Inter's own
2048-unit em space. The placement into this project's 96-unit box — cap height
to 42 units, baseline at y=70, centred on the glyph's own bounding box, y
flipped because a font measures upward and SVG downward — is applied at render
time as an SVG `transform`, NOT baked into the path data. That is what keeps the
claim above checkable, and `src/data/addressMarkerIcons.test.mjs` checks it.

At render time each outline is drawn TWICE by this project: once filled and
stroked dark (a halo, so the letter stays readable over pale terrain) and once
filled white (so Cesium's per-billboard colour multiply can tint it to the
official DPE scale). The halo is deliberately narrower than the rest of the
pack's, because a stroke on an outline grows inward as well as outward.

NO FONT FILE IS REDISTRIBUTED. Only outline data for eight glyphs is vendored,
as path strings inside a JavaScript module. OFL 1.1 §1 permits this; the
reserved-font-name clause (§5) is not engaged, because nothing here is
distributed as a font or under the name Inter.
